+    <div class="article-body">
+<div class="answer-box">
+        <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> A great 10-day Morocco itinerary for a first visit is <strong>Casablanca &rarr; Rabat &rarr; Chefchaouen &rarr; Volubilis &amp; Fes (2 nights) &rarr; the Sahara (2 nights) &rarr; Dades Valley &rarr; Ait Benhaddou &rarr; Marrakech (2&ndash;3 nights)</strong>. It gives you the blue city, both great imperial medinas, a full day in the dunes and time to enjoy Marrakech, without a single day of backtracking. With two weeks, add the Atlantic coast at Essaouira and slow the pace down.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="In this guide">
+        <h2>In this guide</h2>
+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#why-10">Why 10 days is the sweet spot</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#day-by-day">Our recommended 10-day route, day by day</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#alternatives">Two alternative 10-day routes</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#two-weeks">Stretching it to 12&ndash;14 days</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#pace">Getting the pace right</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#cost">What it costs</a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+
+      <p>If you're flying from the US, Canada or Australia, Morocco is a long way to go for a short trip. That's why so many of our guests choose ten days or more. The extra time doesn't just add places to the route. It adds breathing space: a slow breakfast in a riad courtyard, an unplanned stop at a village market, an afternoon with nothing to do but wander.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="why-10">Why 10 Days Is the Sweet Spot</h2>
+      <p>On a <a href="/blog-7-day-morocco-itinerary">7-day itinerary</a>, you have to choose between the north (Fes and Chefchaouen) and a proper amount of time in Marrakech. Ten days removes that choice. It also lets you break the longest drives with interesting stops instead of pushing straight through. Most importantly, nearly every day has something besides time in the vehicle.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="day-by-day">Our Recommended 10-Day Route, Day by Day</h2>
+      <p>This route is built from the legs of our <a href="/tour-11-day-chefchaouen-sahara">11-day Imperial Cities, Chefchaouen &amp; Sahara journey</a>, finishing in Marrakech rather than Essaouira. Drive times are approximate.</p>
+
+      <h3>Day 1: Arrive in Casablanca, on to Rabat</h3>
+      <p>Your driver-guide meets you at Casablanca airport. Rather than spend your first night in a big commercial city, drive about 1h15 to Rabat, Morocco's calm, elegant capital. If you have energy, visit the Hassan Tower and the blue-and-white lanes of the Kasbah des Oudayas at sunset. Our <a href="/blog-rabat-morocco-capital-guide">Rabat guide</a> has more.</p>
+      <h3>Day 2: Rabat to Chefchaouen</h3>
+      <p>About 3h30 north into the Rif Mountains to <a href="/blog-chefchaouen-blue-city-guide">Chefchaouen</a>. Arrive in time for an afternoon in the blue-washed lanes and sunset from the Spanish Mosque on the hill above town.</p>
+      <h3>Day 3: Chefchaouen to Fes via Volubilis and Meknes</h3>
+      <p>Explore the best-preserved Roman ruins in Morocco at <a href="/blog-volubilis-roman-ruins-guide">Volubilis</a>, see the holy hill town of <a href="/blog-moulay-idriss-holy-town-guide">Moulay Idriss</a> from the road, and stop at the monumental gates of Meknes before arriving in Fes (about 3h30 of driving in total).</p>
+      <h3>Day 4: A guided day in Fes</h3>
+      <p>With a licensed local guide, explore Fes el-Bali, one of the largest car-free urban areas in the world: the Bou Inania and Attarine madrasas, the souks, artisans' workshops and the view over the Chouara tannery. Our <a href="/blog-fes-medina-tanneries-guide">Fes medina guide</a> explains what to expect.</p>
+      <h3>Day 5: Fes to the Sahara</h3>
+      <p>The longest drive of the trip, about 6h30, but a beautiful one: the alpine-style town of Ifrane, the <a href="/blog-azrou-cedar-forest-monkeys-guide">Azrou cedar forest</a> and its wild macaques, and the palm-filled <a href="/blog-ziz-valley-oasis-road-guide">Ziz Valley</a>. Swap the vehicle for a camel at the edge of the Erg Chebbi dunes and ride into camp at sunset.</p>
+      <h3>Day 6: A full day in the desert</h3>
+      <p>Explore by 4x4: visit a nomad family for tea, hear Gnawa music in Khamlia, look for flamingos on the seasonal lake (when there's water) and try sandboarding. Back at camp, dinner and a night under the stars. Our guide to <a href="/blog-luxury-desert-camp-merzouga">choosing a luxury desert camp</a> explains your options.</p>
+      <h3>Day 7: Sahara to the Dades Valley</h3>
+      <p>Watch the sunrise from the dunes, then drive about 4h30 west via Rissani and the towering walls of the Todra Gorges to the Dades Valley. Read more in our <a href="/blog-dades-valley-todra-gorge-road-trip">Dades and Todra road-trip guide</a>.</p>
+      <h3>Day 8: Dades Valley to Marrakech</h3>
+      <p>Through the Valley of Roses and Ouarzazate to the fortified village of <a href="/blog-ait-benhaddou-kasbah-guide">Ait Benhaddou</a>, then over the High Atlas by the Tizi n'Tichka pass to Marrakech (about 6h30).</p>
+      <h3>Day 9: Marrakech</h3>
+      <p>A guided morning through the Bahia and Badii palaces, the souks and the Koutoubia, then a free afternoon. Return to <a href="/blog-marrakech-jemaa-el-fnaa-guide">Jemaa el-Fnaa</a> in the evening to watch the square come alive.</p>
+      <h3>Day 10: Free morning and departure</h3>
+      <p>Time for a hammam, a cooking class or last-minute shopping before your transfer to Marrakech airport, or back to Casablanca for flights home.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/badii-palace-marrakech-sunken-garden.jpg" alt="The tall red pisé walls and sunken courtyard of the ruined Badii Palace in Marrakech" width="680" height="907" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>The Badii Palace in Marrakech. Ten days leaves time to explore the city properly, rather than just passing through.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="alternatives">Two Alternative 10-Day Routes</h2>
+      <h3>The desert lover's route: Marrakech, Merzouga and Erg Chigaga</h3>
+      <p>Our <a href="/tour-10-day-ultimate-desert-marrakech">Ultimate Desert Journey</a> (from €2,045 per person) spends two days in Marrakech, then visits <em>both</em> of Morocco's great dune seas: two nights at Merzouga and two nights at a private camp in remote Erg Chigaga, reached by a desert track beyond Zagora. It's ideal if you've been to Fes before, or if the Sahara is the main reason you're coming. Not sure which desert suits you? See our <a href="/blog-merzouga-zagora-erg-chigaga-agafay-which-desert">comparison of Morocco's deserts</a>.</p>
+      <h3>The northern route: Fes, Chefchaouen, the Sahara and Marrakech</h3>
+      <p>Our <a href="/tour-9-day-fes-marrakech-chefchaouen-sahara">9-day journey from Fes</a> (from €1,859 per person) is a good fit if you're flying into Fes via Europe. It includes three nights in Fes with a day trip to Chefchaouen, two desert nights and two nights in Marrakech. Add one extra night anywhere along the route to make it ten days.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="two-weeks">Stretching It to 12&ndash;14 Days</h2>
+      <p>With two weeks, the question shifts from &ldquo;what can we fit in?&rdquo; to &ldquo;where do we want to slow down?&rdquo; The most popular additions are:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>The Atlantic coast (2 nights).</strong> <a href="/blog-essaouira-atlantic-coast-guide">Essaouira</a> is about 2h45 from Marrakech, with a walled medina, a working fishing port and a cool sea breeze. It's the perfect place to unwind at the end of a trip.</li>
+        <li><strong>A night in the High Atlas.</strong> A mountain lodge in the Ourika Valley or the Imlil area means walks among Berber villages and quiet, cool evenings.</li>
+        <li><strong>A second night in Chefchaouen</strong>, so you can see the town early in the morning before the day visitors arrive.</li>
+        <li><strong>A remote desert.</strong> Add Erg Chigaga to Merzouga for a completely different desert experience.</li>
+        <li><strong>A night in the Skoura Oasis</strong> on the way back from the desert, breaking the drive to Marrakech.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>Our <a href="/tour-13-day-imperial-cities-sahara">13-day Grand Loop</a> (from €2,704 per person) and our <a href="/tour-15-day-chefchaouen-sahara-adventure">15-day Grand Tour</a> (from €3,363) are two-week routes that include most of these.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="pace">Getting the Pace Right</h2>
+      <p>The most common mistake we see in self-planned Morocco itineraries is too many one-night stops. A few principles help:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li>Spend at least two nights in Fes and in Marrakech. The medinas reward slow exploring.</li>
+        <li>Never schedule two long driving days back to back if you can avoid it.</li>
+        <li>Give the desert two nights. One night means a rushed sunset and an early start.</li>
+        <li>Leave at least one free half-day in every city.</li>
+        <li>If you're traveling with children or older relatives, add a buffer day. Our <a href="/blog-morocco-family-vacation-with-kids">family travel guide</a> has more ideas.</li>
+      </ul>
+
+      <h2 id="cost">What a 10-Day Private Tour Costs</h2>
+      <p>As a guide, our private journeys of 9 to 11 days cost about <strong>€1,775 to €2,400 per person</strong> for two people sharing a room, and our two-week routes start at about €2,700. The price includes your own driver-guide and air-conditioned vehicle for the whole trip, accommodation, daily breakfast, dinners in the desert and the south, local guides in the cities, and the camel trek. The price per person drops for groups of three or four. Our guide to <a href="/blog-private-morocco-tour-cost">private Morocco tour costs</a> explains the details.</p>
+      <p>Every itinerary above can be adapted to your flights, interests and preferred style of accommodation. <a href="/contact">Tell us about your trip</a>, and we'll design a private 10-day or 2-week itinerary around you.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
