+  <div class="hero-slide-tint"></div>
+  <div class="container">
+    <div class="content-panel">
+    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog">Blog</a> / 7-Day Morocco Itinerary</div>
+    <span class="eyebrow">Itineraries</span>
+    <h1>The Perfect 7-Day Morocco Itinerary: Three Private Routes Compared</h1>
+    <p>Three tried-and-tested one-week routes through the imperial cities, the Atlas Mountains and the Sahara, with real drive times and what to expect each day.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
+
+<section>
+  <div class="container">
+    <div class="article-meta">
+      <span>Itineraries</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>5 min read</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>Morocco Boutique Tours</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <time datetime="2026-11-19">19 November 2026</time>
+    </div>
+    <div class="article-body">
+<div class="answer-box">
+        <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> The best 7-day Morocco itinerary for most first-time visitors is <strong>Casablanca &rarr; Rabat &rarr; Fes &rarr; the Sahara (Merzouga) &rarr; Dades Valley &rarr; Marrakech</strong>. It covers the two great imperial cities, both mountain ranges and two nights in the desert in one direction, with no backtracking. If you're flying in and out of Marrakech, a round trip from Marrakech to the Sahara is more relaxed. Expect three or four longer driving days either way.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="In this guide">
+        <h2>In this guide</h2>
+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#realistic">What's realistic in one week</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#route-1">Route 1: Casablanca to Marrakech via Fes and the Sahara</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#route-2">Route 2: The Grand Sahara loop from Marrakech</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#route-3">Route 3: Marrakech to Casablanca via the Sahara, Fes and Chefchaouen</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#compare">The three routes compared</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#tips">Tips for making one week work</a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+
+      <p>A week in Morocco goes quickly. The country is larger than many visitors expect: Fes to the edge of the Sahara is about 470 km (292 miles), and the road from the desert back to Marrakech crosses the High Atlas. So a good 7-day itinerary is as much about what you leave out as what you include.</p>
+      <p>The three routes below are the ones we recommend most often to travelers from the US, Canada and Australia. Each one is a real private itinerary we run regularly, with real drive times. Each is fully private, with your own driver-guide, so you can adjust the pace, stops and accommodation.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="realistic">What's Realistic in One Week</h2>
+      <p>With seven days, you can comfortably combine:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>One or two imperial cities</strong>, with a guided day in Fes and/or Marrakech</li>
+        <li><strong>A crossing of the High Atlas</strong>, with a stop at the UNESCO-listed kasbah of <a href="/blog-ait-benhaddou-kasbah-guide">Ait Benhaddou</a></li>
+        <li><strong>Two nights in the Sahara</strong>, with a camel trek at sunset and a full day exploring the dunes</li>
+        <li><strong>The gorges and valleys of the south</strong>: the Dades Valley and the Todra Gorges</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>What usually doesn't fit, unless you give up something else: Chefchaouen, Essaouira and the Atlantic coast, or a slow second day in Marrakech. If those matter to you, read our <a href="/blog-10-day-morocco-itinerary">10-day Morocco itinerary</a> instead.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="route-1">Route 1: Casablanca to Marrakech via Fes and the Sahara</h2>
+      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> first-time visitors who want the &ldquo;complete&rdquo; Morocco in one week, and travelers flying into Casablanca from North America.<br><strong>Price:</strong> from €1,504 per person, two sharing. <a href="/tour-7-day-casablanca-fes-sahara">See the full itinerary</a>.</p>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Day by day: 7-day Casablanca to Marrakech via Fes and the Sahara</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Route and highlights</th><th scope="col">Driving</th><th scope="col">Overnight</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Casablanca airport pick-up, the royal capital of Rabat, on to Fes</td><td>~4h15</td><td>Riad in the Fes medina</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Guided day in the Fes medina: the madrasas, souks and Chouara tannery</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Riad in Fes</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Ifrane, the Azrou cedar forest and its Barbary macaques, the Ziz Valley, sunset camel trek</td><td>~6h30</td><td>Desert camp, Merzouga</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>A full desert day by 4x4: nomad families, Gnawa music in Khamlia, the seasonal flamingo lake</td><td>~3h, off-road</td><td>Desert camp, Merzouga</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>Sunrise over the dunes, the Todra Gorges, the Dades Valley</td><td>~4h30</td><td>Kasbah-style hotel, Dades</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">6</th><td>The Valley of Roses, Ait Benhaddou, the Tizi n'Tichka pass</td><td>~6h30</td><td>Riad in Marrakech</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">7</th><td>Guided Marrakech sightseeing, then departure</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p><strong>Why we like it:</strong> it moves in one direction, so you never drive the same road twice, and it gives you a guided day in Fes, Morocco's most fascinating medina. Two nights in the desert mean you get a full day among the dunes rather than arriving at dusk and leaving at dawn.</p>
+      <p><strong>The trade-off:</strong> Marrakech gets only a morning. If you can add one more night, spend it there. Your driver-guide can take you to the airport in Marrakech or back to Casablanca at the end.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="route-2">Route 2: The Grand Sahara Loop from Marrakech</h2>
+      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> travelers flying in and out of Marrakech, couples who want a slower pace, and anyone who wants to focus on the desert and the south.<br><strong>Price:</strong> from €1,260 per person, two sharing. <a href="/tour-7-day-grand-sahara-marrakech">See the full itinerary</a>.</p>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Day by day: 7-day Grand Sahara loop from Marrakech</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Route and highlights</th><th scope="col">Driving</th><th scope="col">Overnight</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>The High Atlas, Ait Benhaddou, Ouarzazate, the Dades Valley</td><td>~6h30</td><td>Kasbah-style hotel, Dades</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>The Todra Gorges, then on to Merzouga for a sunset camel trek</td><td>~4h30</td><td>Luxury desert camp</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>A full desert day: nomads, Gnawa music, the flamingo lake, sandboarding</td><td>~3h, off-road</td><td>Luxury desert camp</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>The fossil workshops of Erfoud, the market town of Rissani, the Skoura Oasis</td><td>~5h</td><td>Kasbah-style hotel, Skoura</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>Telouet Kasbah and the back roads of the High Atlas to the Ourika Valley</td><td>~5h30</td><td>Guesthouse, Ourika Valley</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">6</th><td>A free day in the mountains: waterfall walks at Setti Fatma, or simply rest</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Guesthouse, Ourika Valley</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">7</th><td>Return to Marrakech</td><td>~1h15</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p><strong>Why we like it:</strong> the driving is spread more evenly than on most desert routes, and the nights in Skoura and the Ourika Valley give the week a calm, unhurried second half. It's also the best-value 7-day route, because there are no city guides or medina riads to pay for.</p>
+      <p><strong>The trade-off:</strong> you won't see Fes. Most travelers on this route add two or three nights in Marrakech before or after.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/guide-abdul.jpg" alt="Morocco Boutique Tours driver-guide Abdul in a blue robe and white turban, sitting against a stone wall" width="680" height="1209" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>Abdul, one of our private driver-guides. On a one-week trip, a guide who knows where to stop, and for how long, makes a real difference.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="route-3">Route 3: Marrakech to Casablanca via the Sahara, Fes and Chefchaouen</h2>
+      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> photographers and travelers who don't want to miss the Blue City, especially if your flight home leaves from Casablanca.<br><strong>Price:</strong> from €1,420 per person, two sharing. <a href="/tour-7-day-marrakech-casablanca-fes">See the full itinerary</a>.</p>
+      <p>This route runs the classic desert crossing in reverse: Marrakech to the Dades Valley, two nights in the Sahara, then north through the Ziz Valley and the cedar forests to Fes. After a guided day in Fes, you continue via Meknes and the Roman ruins of <a href="/blog-volubilis-roman-ruins-guide">Volubilis</a> to <a href="/blog-chefchaouen-blue-city-guide">Chefchaouen</a>, and finish in Casablanca via Rabat.</p>
+      <p><strong>Why we like it:</strong> it fits the most variety into a week, from the dunes to the blue lanes of the Rif Mountains. <strong>The trade-off:</strong> it's the busiest of the three routes, with only one night in Chefchaouen and no time for Marrakech itself unless you arrive a day or two early.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="compare">The Three Routes Compared</h2>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Comparison of three 7-day Morocco itineraries</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Route 1: Casablanca &rarr; Marrakech</th><th scope="col">Route 2: Marrakech loop</th><th scope="col">Route 3: Marrakech &rarr; Casablanca</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Imperial cities</th><td>Rabat, Fes, Marrakech</td><td>Marrakech (start and end)</td><td>Fes, Meknes, Rabat</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Desert nights</th><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Chefchaouen</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, one night</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Pace</th><td>Full</td><td>Most relaxed</td><td>Busiest</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">From (pp, 2 sharing)</th><td>€1,504</td><td>€1,260</td><td>€1,420</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+
+      <h2 id="tips">Tips for Making One Week Work</h2>
+      <ol>
+        <li><strong>Arrive the night before if you can.</strong> Flights from North America and Australia are long. A night in Casablanca or Marrakech before Day 1 means you start the tour rested.</li>
+        <li><strong>Fly in and out of different cities.</strong> An open-jaw ticket (into Casablanca, out of Marrakech, for example) saves a long return drive.</li>
+        <li><strong>Don't cut the desert to one night.</strong> One night means arriving at sunset and leaving after sunrise. A second night gives you a whole day in the dunes, and it's often the part of the trip people remember most.</li>
+        <li><strong>Upgrade where it matters.</strong> For many couples, that's a <a href="/blog-luxury-desert-camp-merzouga">luxury desert camp</a> or a special riad in Marrakech.</li>
+        <li><strong>Travel in spring or autumn</strong> if you can. See our guide to the <a href="/blog-best-time-to-visit-morocco">best time to visit Morocco</a>.</li>
+      </ol>
+      <p>Every route here can be adjusted. You might add a night in Marrakech, swap the Ourika Valley for Essaouira, or start in Fes instead of Casablanca. Browse all our <a href="/tours">Signature Journeys</a>, or <a href="/tailor-made">design a tailor-made week</a> around your own flights and interests.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
