+    <div class="article-body">
+      <div class="faq-category">
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Is 7 days enough for Morocco?</summary>
+          <p>Seven days is enough for a rewarding first trip that includes one imperial city, the High Atlas Mountains and two nights in the Sahara, or two cities and one full desert day. It isn't enough to see everything, so most travelers leave Chefchaouen, Essaouira or a second city for a longer trip.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>What is the best 7-day route in Morocco for first-timers?</summary>
+          <p>For travelers flying into Casablanca, the most complete one-week route is Casablanca to Rabat and Fes, then across the Middle Atlas to the Sahara, and back over the High Atlas to Marrakech. If you fly in and out of Marrakech, a round trip to the Sahara via Ait Benhaddou and the Dades Valley is more relaxed.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Can you see Marrakech, Fes and the Sahara in one week?</summary>
+          <p>Yes, but it's a full week with three long driving days. Our 7-day Casablanca to Marrakech route covers all three, with a guided day in Fes, two nights in the desert and a guided morning in Marrakech.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>How much does a 7-day private tour of Morocco cost?</summary>
+          <p>Our 7-day private journeys cost from €1,260 to €1,504 per person for two people sharing, depending on the route. That includes your own driver-guide and vehicle, accommodation, daily breakfast, dinners in the desert and the Dades Valley, and the camel trek.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Should I fly into Casablanca or Marrakech?</summary>
+          <p>From North America, Casablanca usually has the most direct flights, with nonstop services from cities including New York and Montreal. Marrakech is easier if you're connecting through Europe. Many travelers fly into one and out of the other, which avoids backtracking.</p>
+        </details>
