+    <span class="eyebrow">Planning</span>
+    <h1>The Best Time to Visit Morocco: A Month-by-Month Guide</h1>
+    <p>Weather, crowds and festivals across the whole country, from the cities and mountains to the coast and the Sahara, plus the best months for North American and Australian holidays.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
+
+<section>
+  <div class="container">
+    <div class="article-meta">
+      <span>Planning</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>6 min read</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>Morocco Boutique Tours</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <time datetime="2026-11-19">19 November 2026</time>
+    </div>
+    <div class="article-body">
+<div class="answer-box">
+        <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> The best time to visit Morocco is <strong>spring (March to May)</strong> or <strong>autumn (late September to November)</strong>. These months bring warm, mostly dry days across the whole country, comfortable conditions in the Sahara and pleasant evenings in Marrakech and Fes. Winter (December to February) is mild and sunny by day but cold at night. Summer (June to August) is very hot inland, so trips then work best when they lean on the Atlantic coast and the mountains.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="In this guide">
+        <h2>In this guide</h2>
+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#regions">Why the answer depends on where you go</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#month-by-month">Morocco month by month</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#seasons">The four seasons at a glance</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#holidays">Best times for US, Canadian and Australian holidays</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#festivals">Festivals worth planning around</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#ramadan">Traveling during Ramadan</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#booking">When to book</a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+
+      <p>Morocco is a year-round destination, but it's also a country of very different climates. In a single week you can drive from the cool Atlantic breezes of Essaouira, over High Atlas passes that see snow in winter, to the edge of the Sahara. So the right month depends less on Morocco as a whole and more on the route you have in mind. This guide looks at the whole country. If the desert is your main focus, our separate guide to the <a href="/blog-best-time-to-visit-sahara-desert">best time to visit the Sahara</a> goes into more detail.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="regions">Why the Answer Depends on Where You Go</h2>
+      <p>Most private tours combine four kinds of landscape, and each has its own best season:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>Imperial cities (Marrakech, Fes, Meknes, Rabat).</strong> Hot summers and mild winters. Best from March to May and from mid-September to November.</li>
+        <li><strong>The Sahara (Merzouga, Erg Chigaga).</strong> Extreme summer heat and cold winter nights. Best in spring and autumn.</li>
+        <li><strong>The High and Middle Atlas.</strong> Snow is possible from December to March on high passes. Summer is pleasantly fresh.</li>
+        <li><strong>The Atlantic coast (Essaouira, Rabat, Casablanca).</strong> Mild all year, rarely hot, often windy in summer.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>A classic first-time route links all four, which is why spring and autumn are so popular: they're the only seasons when everywhere on the route is comfortable at the same time.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="month-by-month">Morocco Month by Month</h2>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Typical weather in Marrakech and the Sahara by month</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Marrakech daytime</th><th scope="col">Sahara (Merzouga)</th><th scope="col">Our verdict</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">January</th><td>Mild, ~64&ndash;66°F (18&ndash;19°C)</td><td>Sunny days, near-freezing nights</td><td>Good, quiet, pack warm layers</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">February</th><td>Mild, ~68°F (20°C)</td><td>Cool nights, clear skies</td><td>Good; almond blossom in the south</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">March</th><td>Warm, ~72°F (22°C)</td><td>Comfortable</td><td>Excellent</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">April</th><td>Warm, ~77°F (25°C)</td><td>Ideal</td><td>Excellent; one of the best months</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">May</th><td>Warm to hot, ~84°F (29°C)</td><td>Hot by midday</td><td>Very good; roses in the Dades Valley</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">June</th><td>Hot, ~91°F (33°C)</td><td>Very hot</td><td>Fair; favor the coast and mountains</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">July</th><td>Very hot, ~98°F (37°C)</td><td>Extreme, often 110°F+ (43°C+)</td><td>Challenging inland</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">August</th><td>Very hot, ~98°F (37°C)</td><td>Extreme</td><td>Challenging inland</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">September</th><td>Hot, easing, ~90°F (32°C)</td><td>Hot early, better late</td><td>Good from mid-month</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">October</th><td>Warm, ~82°F (28°C)</td><td>Ideal</td><td>Excellent; one of the best months</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">November</th><td>Mild, ~72°F (22°C)</td><td>Comfortable days, cool nights</td><td>Excellent</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">December</th><td>Mild, ~66°F (19°C)</td><td>Sunny days, cold nights</td><td>Good; busy over Christmas</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p class="table-note">Temperatures are typical average daytime highs, rounded. Individual days can be several degrees warmer or cooler.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="seasons">The Four Seasons at a Glance</h2>
+      <h3>Spring (March to May): the classic choice</h3>
+      <p>Spring is Morocco at its greenest. The fields around Fes and Meknes are bright with wheat and wildflowers, the Atlas peaks still carry a little snow for photographs, and the Sahara is warm without being hot. It's ideal for a first trip that links the imperial cities, the mountains and the desert. Easter and the spring-break weeks are busy, so book desert camps and riads early.</p>
+      <h3>Summer (June to August): coast and mountains</h3>
+      <p>Inland Morocco is very hot in summer. Marrakech regularly reaches the high 90s °F (around 37°C) and the Sahara can pass 113°F (45°C). Summer travel still works if you plan around it. Spend more time in breezy Essaouira, stay in the High Atlas where nights are cool, choose riads with plunge pools, and time desert activities for sunrise and sunset. Families tied to school holidays often travel this way.</p>
+      <h3>Autumn (late September to November): our favorite</h3>
+      <p>After the summer heat breaks, the country settles into long, golden days. The date harvest begins in the southern oases, the desert camps are in full swing, and the light over the dunes is beautiful. October and November are among our busiest months for private tours, and for good reason.</p>
+      <h3>Winter (December to February): sun, snow and clear skies</h3>
+      <p>Winter days are usually sunny and pleasant in Marrakech, but nights get cold, and riads aren't always well heated. In the desert, the night sky is at its clearest, and nights at camp can approach freezing. Snow occasionally closes the Tizi n'Tichka pass for a few hours. For a detailed look, see our guide to <a href="/blog-morocco-in-winter-guide">Morocco in winter</a>.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/dades-rock-formations.jpg" alt="Folded red and ochre rock formations above a strip of green fields in the Dades Valley" width="680" height="383" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>The Dades Valley in autumn. Spring and autumn are the only seasons when the cities, mountains and desert are all comfortable at once.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="holidays">The Best Times for US, Canadian and Australian Holidays</h2>
+      <p>For long-haul travelers, the best time to visit Morocco often comes down to when you can actually take the time off. Here's how the usual holiday windows match up:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>Spring break and Canada's March Break.</strong> Excellent timing. March is warm by day across the country, and the Sahara is comfortable. These weeks are popular, so book at least three to four months ahead.</li>
+        <li><strong>Easter.</strong> Ideal weather, but one of the busiest periods of the year in Morocco, with many European visitors too.</li>
+        <li><strong>US and Canadian summer vacation.</strong> Hot inland. Build in the coast and mountains, keep driving days short, and plan desert time for early morning and evening.</li>
+        <li><strong>Canadian Thanksgiving (October) and US Thanksgiving (late November).</strong> Both are superb. Late November is one of our favorite times of year: mild, golden and quieter than spring.</li>
+        <li><strong>Christmas and New Year.</strong> Sunny days and festive riads, but cold desert nights and the year's highest demand. Desert camps for New Year's Eve often fill up months ahead.</li>
+        <li><strong>Australian summer school holidays (December&ndash;January).</strong> Remember that this is Morocco's winter. Days are mild and sunny, but pack proper layers for the evenings.</li>
+        <li><strong>Australian April and September&ndash;October school holidays.</strong> Close to perfect for a family trip that includes the Sahara.</li>
+      </ul>
+
+      <h2 id="festivals">Festivals Worth Planning Around</h2>
+      <p>Dates move from year to year, so confirm them before you book flights, but these regular events are worth knowing about:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>Almond blossom (February).</strong> The Anti-Atlas around Tafraoute turns white and pink.</li>
+        <li><strong>Rose Festival, Kelaat M'Gouna (usually May).</strong> The Dades Valley celebrates the Damask rose harvest.</li>
+        <li><strong>Fes Festival of World Sacred Music (usually late spring).</strong> Concerts in historic venues across Fes.</li>
+        <li><strong>Gnaoua and World Music Festival, Essaouira (usually June).</strong> Free open-air concerts and a lively crowd. Book accommodation very early.</li>
+        <li><strong>Imilchil marriage festival (usually September).</strong> A traditional gathering of Amazigh (Berber) tribes in the High Atlas.</li>
+        <li><strong>Erfoud Date Festival (usually October).</strong> The harvest celebrated in the oasis on the road to Merzouga.</li>
+      </ul>
+
+      <h2 id="ramadan">Traveling During Ramadan</h2>
+      <p>During the holy month of Ramadan, many Moroccans fast from dawn until sunset. Ramadan 2027 is expected to begin around <strong>8 February</strong> and end around <strong>9 March</strong>, depending on the sighting of the moon. Visitors aren't expected to fast, and private tours run normally. Hotels, riads and desert camps serve meals as usual. Some local cafés close during the day, and the late afternoon can be quieter, but the evenings, when families break their fast together, have a special atmosphere. Eating and drinking discreetly in public during daylight is a thoughtful courtesy. Note that Morocco usually moves its clocks back by one hour for Ramadan, which can affect flight connections.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="booking">When to Book</h2>
+      <p>For spring, autumn and the Christmas&ndash;New Year period, we recommend booking a private tour <strong>three to six months ahead</strong>. The best riads and desert camps have a limited number of rooms and tents, and they go first. For quieter months, a few weeks' notice is often enough, but earlier booking always gives you more choice. Our guide to <a href="/blog-private-morocco-tour-cost">how much a private Morocco tour costs</a> explains how the time of year affects the price.</p>
+      <p>Whenever you travel, a private itinerary lets you shape each day around the weather: an early start before the heat, a longer lunch in the shade, or an extra night where it's most comfortable. Browse our <a href="/tours">Signature Journeys</a> for ideas, or <a href="/contact">tell us your dates</a> and we'll suggest the route that suits them best.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
