+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#advisories">What the US, Canadian and Australian governments say</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#real-risks">The risks you're actually likely to meet</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#scams">Common scams and how to avoid them</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#women">Is Morocco safe for women?</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#families">Families, older travelers and LGBTQ+ visitors</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#health">Health, food and water</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#roads">Roads, driving and the desert</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#private">How a private tour changes the experience</a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+
+      <p>&ldquo;Is Morocco safe?&rdquo; is the question we hear most often from travelers in the United States, Canada and Australia. It's usually asked by someone who has already fallen for the photographs of Chefchaouen and the Sahara, but has also read a worrying forum thread or a headline. That's a sensible question to ask before booking a long-haul trip.</p>
+      <p>We're a local Moroccan company, so we're not neutral. This guide is honest anyway. It covers what governments say, what really happens on the ground, and what you can do to travel calmly and confidently.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="advisories">What the US, Canadian and Australian Governments Say</h2>
+      <p>Government travel advice is the best place to start, because it's written for your passport and updated when things change. Here's a summary at the time of writing (November 2026):</p>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Government travel advice for Morocco by country</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col">Government</th><th scope="col">Overall advice for Morocco</th><th scope="col">Main reason given</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">United States (State Department)</th><td>Level 2: Exercise increased caution</td><td>Risk of terrorism</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Canada (Global Affairs Canada)</th><td>Exercise a high degree of caution; avoid all travel to the Western Sahara border regions</td><td>Threat of terrorism, plus crime in some areas</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Australia (Smartraveller)</th><td>Check the colored advice level on the Morocco page</td><td>See the current page</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p class="table-note">Advice changes. Always read the current pages: <a href="https://travel.state.gov/en/international-travel/travel-advisories/morocco.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">travel.state.gov</a>, <a href="https://travel.gc.ca/destinations/morocco" target="_blank" rel="noopener">travel.gc.ca</a> and <a href="https://www.smartraveller.gov.au/destinations/africa/morocco" target="_blank" rel="noopener">smartraveller.gov.au</a>.</p>
+      <p>Two points are worth understanding. First, &ldquo;increased caution&rdquo; is a common rating. The US gives it to many European countries that Americans visit without a second thought. Second, the areas governments tell you to avoid are remote border zones near Algeria and the Western Sahara berm. No classic tourist itinerary goes near them. Marrakech, Fes, Chefchaouen, the Atlas Mountains, the Dades Valley, Merzouga and the Atlantic coast are all well away from those areas.</p>
+      <p>Before you travel, enroll in your government's traveler registration service (the STEP program for US citizens, Registration of Canadians Abroad, or Smartraveller for Australians), take out travel insurance and keep a copy of your passport somewhere separate from the original.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="real-risks">The Risks You're Actually Likely to Meet</h2>
+      <p>In our experience, almost nobody's trip is spoiled by serious crime. When something goes wrong, it's usually one of these:</p>
+      <h3>Persistent selling and attention in the medinas</h3>
+      <p>The old medinas of Marrakech and Fes are working markets, and shopkeepers are skilled at drawing you in. To a first-time visitor that can feel intense. It's rarely aggressive, though. A smile, a polite &ldquo;la, shukran&rdquo; (no, thank you) and walking on is usually enough.</p>
+      <h3>Unofficial &ldquo;guides&rdquo;</h3>
+      <p>Young men may offer to show you the way, tell you a road is closed, or say the tanneries are &ldquo;this way&rdquo;. Unlicensed guiding is illegal in Morocco, and these encounters usually end at a shop that pays commission or with a demand for money. Official guides carry an identification badge. The simplest fix is to book a licensed local guide for your first day in Fes or Marrakech. Once you know the layout, the medina feels much less overwhelming.</p>
+      <h3>Pickpocketing and bag snatching</h3>
+      <p>As in Barcelona or Rome, busy places such as Jemaa el-Fnaa square attract pickpockets. Keep phones out of back pockets, wear a cross-body bag on the front, and don't hang a camera loosely over your shoulder in a crowd. Use the safe in your riad for passports and spare cash.</p>
+      <h3>Traffic</h3>
+      <p>For many visitors, crossing the road is the most dangerous thing they do all trip. Scooters weave through medina lanes and city traffic follows its own rhythm. Cross with locals, look both ways even on one-way streets, and step aside when you hear &ldquo;balak!&rdquo; (watch out) behind you in the souk.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="scams">Common Scams and How to Avoid Them</h2>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>&ldquo;The square is closed&rdquo; or &ldquo;that way is only for Muslims&rdquo;.</strong> Usually untrue, and designed to lead you elsewhere. Check your map and carry on.</li>
+        <li><strong>Henna in Jemaa el-Fnaa.</strong> Someone grabs your hand and starts drawing, then asks for a large fee. Keep your hands to yourself and say no firmly before it starts.</li>
+        <li><strong>Photos of performers and animals.</strong> Snake charmers, monkey handlers and costumed water sellers expect payment for photos. Agree a price first, or don't take the picture. We'd also gently encourage you not to pay for photos with chained monkeys.</li>
+        <li><strong>Taxis without meters.</strong> City petit taxis should use the meter. If the driver refuses, agree the fare before you get in, or find another taxi. With a private driver, the question never comes up.</li>
+        <li><strong>The &ldquo;festival&rdquo; or &ldquo;last day of the Berber market&rdquo;.</strong> A friendly stranger tells you there's something special happening today, and it turns out to be a carpet shop.</li>
+      </ul>
+
+      <h2 id="women">Is Morocco Safe for Women?</h2>
+      <p>Women travel throughout Morocco every day, with partners, friends, family or alone. The most common complaint isn't danger but attention: comments, whistles and occasional persistence from young men in busy medinas. A few practical things make a real difference:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li>Dress modestly, especially outside Marrakech's newer districts. Covering your shoulders and knees isn't required by law, but it reduces attention noticeably. A light scarf is useful in mosques and rural villages.</li>
+        <li>Walk with purpose, and don't feel obliged to answer every greeting.</li>
+        <li>Avoid deserted medina lanes after dark. Your riad can send someone to meet you at the nearest square.</li>
+        <li>Choose well-reviewed riads with staff on site day and night.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>Many of the women who travel with us tell us the hassle almost disappears when they're with a guide, because local people treat you as a guest rather than a potential customer.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="families">Families, Older Travelers and LGBTQ+ Visitors</h2>
+      <h3>Families with children</h3>
+      <p>Moroccan culture is warm towards children, and families with kids are often greeted with real affection. The main practical concerns are heat, long drives and food. We cover those in detail in our guide to <a href="/blog-morocco-family-vacation-with-kids">planning a Morocco family vacation</a>.</p>
+      <h3>Older travelers</h3>
+      <p>Medinas involve uneven cobbles, steps and long walks, and riads often have steep stairs. A private tour lets you set a gentle pace, ask for ground-floor rooms and keep sightseeing to the cooler morning hours.</p>
+      <h3>LGBTQ+ travelers</h3>
+      <p>Same-sex relations are illegal under Moroccan law, and public displays of affection of any kind are frowned upon. In practice, same-sex couples do visit Morocco and stay in riads and hotels without incident, especially when they keep a low profile in public. Read your government's advice for LGBTQ+ travelers before deciding, and if you travel with us, tell us privately what you'd like us to keep in mind.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/guide-hassan.jpg" alt="Morocco Boutique Tours driver-guide Hassan standing beside his private vehicle with the Hassan II Mosque minaret behind him in Casablanca" width="680" height="907" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>Hassan, one of our private driver-guides, in Casablanca. A guide who knows the country well is the best safety measure there is.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="health">Health, Food and Water</h2>
+      <p>The most likely health issue on a Morocco trip is an upset stomach, not a serious illness. To reduce the risk:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li>Drink bottled or filtered water, and be careful with ice from street stalls.</li>
+        <li>Eat freshly cooked hot food. Tagines and grills cooked in front of you are usually a safe choice.</li>
+        <li>Wash or sanitize your hands before eating, and pack a basic travel medicine kit.</li>
+        <li>Take the sun seriously, even in spring. Desert and mountain sun is strong, and dehydration builds up on long driving days.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>Private clinics and pharmacies in the big cities are good, and pharmacists are a useful first stop for minor problems. For anything serious, travel insurance with medical evacuation cover is essential. Check routine vaccinations with your doctor or travel clinic before you go.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="roads">Roads, Driving and the Desert</h2>
+      <p>Morocco's main roads are generally in good condition, but long distances, mountain passes such as the Tizi n'Tichka, livestock on rural roads and night driving all call for experience. If you're unsure about self-driving, a private driver-guide is the safer and more relaxing option. Our guide to <a href="/blog-morocco-private-driver-guide">hiring a private driver in Morocco</a> explains how it works.</p>
+      <p>In the Sahara, the main risks are the heat, the sun and getting lost on your own. Established camps at Merzouga and Erg Chigaga are staffed, and camel treks and 4x4 excursions are led by people who know the dunes. Don't wander off into the dunes alone at night, and drink plenty of water during the day.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="private">How a Private Tour Changes the Experience</h2>
+      <p>No tour can remove every risk, and we'd never claim otherwise. But traveling with your own local driver-guide reduces most of the everyday friction that worries first-time visitors:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li>You're met at the airport and never have to negotiate taxis or find a riad hidden in the medina.</li>
+        <li>A licensed local guide takes you through the medinas, so unofficial guides leave you alone.</li>
+        <li>Someone who speaks the local languages, and English, is beside you if anything goes wrong.</li>
+        <li>Your route, accommodation and driving times are planned by people who drive them every week.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>That's one of the main reasons families, couples and older travelers from North America and Australia choose a <a href="/tours">private Morocco tour</a> over traveling independently for a first visit. If you'd like to see how it works in practice, read about <a href="/our-story">our team and how we work</a>, or <a href="/contact">send us your questions</a>. We reply personally, and we're always happy to talk through any concerns before you book.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
+
+<section class="section-sand" id="faq">
+  <div class="container">
+    <div class="section-head left" style="max-width:720px; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;">
+      <span class="eyebrow">Frequently Asked</span>
+      <h2>Morocco Safety: Quick Answers</h2>
+    </div>
+    <div class="article-body">
+      <div class="faq-category">
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Is Morocco safe for American tourists?</summary>
+          <p>Yes, for most visitors. The US State Department rates Morocco Level 2, "Exercise increased caution", the same level it gives many European countries, mainly because of a general risk of terrorism. Millions of international visitors travel each year without problems. The everyday issues Americans are most likely to meet are pushy selling, unofficial guides and petty theft in busy medinas.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>What do Canada and Australia say about travel to Morocco?</summary>
+          <p>Canada advises travelers to exercise a high degree of caution in Morocco, mainly because of the threat of terrorism, and to avoid all travel to the Western Sahara border regions. Australia's Smartraveller publishes its own advice level for Morocco. Check both sites for the current wording before you travel, and register your trip with your government's traveler service.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Is Morocco safe for women travelers?</summary>
+          <p>Women travel in Morocco every day, alone and in groups. Unwanted comments and attention in medinas are the most common complaint. Dressing modestly, walking with purpose, ignoring persistent approaches and avoiding empty medina lanes late at night all help. Traveling with a private driver-guide removes most of this friction.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Is it safe to go to the Sahara Desert in Morocco?</summary>
+          <p>Yes. The Merzouga and Erg Chigaga dune areas used by tour operators are well-established destinations with staffed camps. The areas governments advise against are the remote border zones with Algeria and the Western Sahara berm, which are far from normal tourist routes.</p>
+        </details>
+        <details class="faq-item">
+          <summary>Can you drink the tap water in Morocco?</summary>
+          <p>Tap water in the main cities is treated, but most visitors stick to bottled or filtered water to avoid upset stomachs. Bottled water is cheap and sold everywhere, and your driver-guide will keep the vehicle stocked.</p>
+        </details>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
