+    <span class="eyebrow">Desert</span>
+    <h1>Choosing a Luxury Desert Camp in Merzouga: What “Luxury” Really Means</h1>
+    <p>Tents, bathrooms, location, food and season: the questions that separate a genuinely comfortable Sahara camp from a disappointing one.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
+
+<section>
+  <div class="container">
+    <div class="article-meta">
+      <span>Desert</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>5 min read</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <span>Morocco Boutique Tours</span>
+      <span>&middot;</span>
+      <time datetime="2026-11-19">19 November 2026</time>
+    </div>
+    <div class="article-body">
+<div class="answer-box">
+        <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> A real luxury desert camp in Merzouga gives you a <strong>large private tent with a proper bed and an en-suite bathroom</strong> (hot shower and flush toilet), set <strong>within the Erg Chebbi dunes</strong> rather than on the edge of town, with good food, attentive staff and enough space between tents to feel peaceful. Before booking, confirm five things: the bathroom, the location, the number of tents, heating or cooling for your season, and how you'll reach the camp.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="In this guide">
+        <h2>In this guide</h2>
+        <ol>
+          <li><a href="#levels">Standard, luxury and private camps: the differences</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#checklist">What to check before you book</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#season">How the season changes your choice</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#merzouga-vs-chigaga">Merzouga or Erg Chigaga?</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#upgrades">Small touches worth asking for</a></li>
+          <li><a href="#questions">Questions to ask your tour operator</a></li>
+        </ol>
+      </nav>
+
+      <p>The night in the Sahara is the moment most travelers look forward to before a Morocco trip, and the one they talk about most afterwards. It's also the part of the trip where quality varies most. &ldquo;Luxury camp&rdquo; appears on countless listings around Merzouga, and it can mean anything from a genuinely beautiful tented retreat to a row of tents beside a car park on the edge of the dunes.</p>
+      <p>We take guests into the Erg Chebbi dunes throughout the year, so this guide is about what actually matters when you choose. For a feel of the evening itself, from the camel ride in to the campfire, read <a href="/blog-night-in-sahara-desert-camp">a night in the Sahara: what to expect</a>.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="levels">Standard, Luxury and Private Camps: the Differences</h2>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table>
+          <caption class="visually-hidden">Comparison of standard, luxury and private desert camps in Merzouga</caption>
+          <thead>
+            <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Standard camp</th><th scope="col">Luxury camp</th><th scope="col">Private or exclusive camp</th></tr>
+          </thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Tent</th><td>Simple tent, basic beds</td><td>Large furnished tent, proper bed, quality linen</td><td>As luxury, often larger, with a private lounge or terrace</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Bathroom</th><td>Often shared</td><td>Private en-suite with hot shower</td><td>Private en-suite</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Setting</th><td>Often near the edge of the dunes</td><td>Usually within the dunes</td><td>Usually secluded</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Dining</th><td>Set menu, communal</td><td>Multi-course dinner, better service</td><td>Private dining on request</td></tr>
+            <tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Budget and backpacker trips</td><td>Most couples and families</td><td>Honeymoons, celebrations, groups of friends</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+      <p>On our itineraries, the desert nights are at established luxury camps in the Erg Chebbi dunes, such as Tiziri Camp or Sahara Eden Camp, with private furnished tents and, in most cases, en-suite bathrooms. If you'd like something more exclusive, we can suggest options for your dates.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/campfire-group-music.jpg" alt="Guests and musicians gathered on rugs around a campfire at a Merzouga desert camp at night, with tents lit up behind them" width="680" height="510" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>Evenings at camp center on the fire. A well-run camp feels sociable here and private once you return to your tent.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="checklist">What to Check Before You Book</h2>
+      <h3>1. The bathroom</h3>
+      <p>This is the single biggest difference between camp levels. Ask whether each tent has its own en-suite bathroom, whether there's hot water, and whether the toilet is a flush toilet. Most good luxury camps now offer all three, but don't assume.</p>
+      <h3>2. The location</h3>
+      <p>Some camps sit right on the edge of the dunes, a short walk from the road. They're easy to reach, but you'll hear vehicles and see lights. Camps set further into Erg Chebbi feel far more remote, and the camel ride in is longer and more memorable. Ask how far the camp is from the nearest road and what the view from the tents looks like.</p>
+      <h3>3. The size of the camp</h3>
+      <p>A camp with a dozen tents feels very different from one with fifty. Smaller camps are quieter, the service is more personal and the campfire evening feels like a gathering rather than a show. Ask how many tents there are and how far apart they're spaced.</p>
+      <h3>4. Beds, lighting and power</h3>
+      <p>Look for real beds with mattresses (not mats on the floor), good linen and blankets, and lighting inside the tent. Many camps run on solar power. There's usually somewhere to charge a phone, but don't count on Wi-Fi, and phone signal can be patchy.</p>
+      <h3>5. Getting there</h3>
+      <p>Most guests arrive by camel at sunset, which is part of the magic. Ask how long the ride is, and whether there's a 4x4 alternative for anyone who can't or doesn't want to ride. That's useful for young children, older travelers and anyone with back problems. You'll normally take a small overnight bag to camp, while your main luggage stays safely in your vehicle.</p>
+      <h3>6. Food</h3>
+      <p>A good camp serves a proper dinner: soup or salads, a tagine or couscous, fruit and mint tea, often with Berber bread baked in the sand. Tell your operator about dietary needs in advance. Vegetarian, gluten-free and child-friendly meals are all possible with notice.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="season">How the Season Changes Your Choice</h2>
+      <p>Temperature matters more in the Sahara than almost anywhere else on a Morocco trip:</p>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>November to February:</strong> sunny, pleasant days but nights that can approach freezing. Choose a camp with heavy blankets and ask whether tents are heated. A hot shower suddenly matters a lot.</li>
+        <li><strong>March to May and late September to October:</strong> the most comfortable months. Almost any good camp will be lovely.</li>
+        <li><strong>June to August:</strong> intense heat by day, warm nights. Ask about fans or air-conditioning, and plan to spend the middle of the day somewhere shaded or at a hotel with a pool.</li>
+      </ul>
+      <p>For more on timing, see our guide to the <a href="/blog-best-time-to-visit-sahara-desert">best time to visit the Sahara</a>.</p>
+
+      <h2 id="merzouga-vs-chigaga">Merzouga or Erg Chigaga?</h2>
+      <p>Morocco has two great dune seas with luxury camps. <strong>Merzouga's Erg Chebbi</strong> is reached by paved road from both Fes and Marrakech, has the widest choice of camps and is surrounded by interesting excursions such as nomad families, Khamlia's Gnawa musicians and the seasonal flamingo lake. <strong>Erg Chigaga</strong>, beyond Zagora, is wilder and quieter, with far fewer camps, but the final stretch is a long track that needs a 4x4. Couples who want real seclusion often love it. Our <a href="/blog-merzouga-zagora-erg-chigaga-agafay-which-desert">comparison of Morocco's deserts</a> covers both in detail, along with why Agafay, near Marrakech, isn't the same thing as the Sahara.</p>
+
+      <figure>
+        <img src="images/guide-hamid-camel.jpg" alt="Hamid, a Sahara camel guide in a blue robe and white turban, standing beside a camel loaded with blankets in the Merzouga dunes" width="680" height="453" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
+        <figcaption>Hamid, our camel guide in the Sahara. The team on the ground matters as much as the tents themselves.</figcaption>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h2 id="upgrades">Small Touches Worth Asking For</h2>
+      <ul>
+        <li><strong>A second night.</strong> The best upgrade of all. You get a full day in the dunes and the camp to enjoy in daylight.</li>
+        <li><strong>A private dinner</strong> set up on the sand away from the main tent, lovely for an anniversary or a honeymoon.</li>
+        <li><strong>Sunrise from the top of a dune.</strong> Ask your camel guide to wake you early and walk you up for first light.</li>
+        <li><strong>A family tent</strong> or connecting tents if you're traveling with children.</li>
+        <li><strong>Stargazing.</strong> Away from town lights, the Milky Way is often visible to the naked eye. Some camps have a telescope, or your guide can point out the constellations.</li>
+      </ul>
+
+      <h2 id="questions">Questions to Ask Your Tour Operator</h2>
+      <p>Whoever you book with, these questions will tell you a lot:</p>
+      <ol>
+        <li>What is the name of the camp, and can I see photos of the actual tents?</li>
+        <li>Does each tent have a private bathroom with a hot shower and flush toilet?</li>
+        <li>How far is the camp from the road, and how many tents does it have?</li>
+        <li>What's provided for cold nights (or hot days) on my dates?</li>
+        <li>How do we get to camp, and is there an alternative to the camel?</li>
+        <li>Is dinner included, and can you cater for our dietary needs?</li>
+      </ol>
+      <p>A good operator will answer every one of these without hesitating. Our itinerary pages name the camps we use, so you can look them up yourself. Browse our <a href="/tours">Signature Journeys</a> to see where each itinerary stays, or <a href="/contact">ask us about desert camps for your dates</a>.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</section>
