       <h3>4-5 Days</h3>
       <p>Adding a day (or two, on the Erg Chigaga route via Zagora) buys you a full day exploring the dunes by 4x4 — visiting nomadic families, hearing Gnawa music in Khamlia, and sandboarding — rather than just an overnight stop. This is our most commonly recommended length for a first Sahara trip.</p>
       <h3>7+ Days</h3>
-      <p>Longer itineraries fold in more of northern Morocco — Fes, Chefchaouen, Meknes, Casablanca, or the Atlantic coast at Essaouira — turning the desert crossing into one leg of a much fuller Moroccan circuit.</p>
+      <p>Longer itineraries fold in more of northern Morocco — Fes, Chefchaouen, Meknes, Casablanca, or the Atlantic coast at Essaouira — turning the desert crossing into one leg of a much fuller Moroccan circuit. For route ideas, see our <a href="/blog-7-day-morocco-itinerary">7-day</a> and <a href="/blog-10-day-morocco-itinerary">10-day Morocco itineraries</a>.</p>
 
       <h2>A Simple Way to Decide</h2>
       <p>If you have 3 days or less to spare and just want a genuine taste of the Sahara, choose a 3-day round trip. If the desert is a priority and you have a bit more time, a 4 or 5-day tour gives you a full day among the dunes rather than a quick overnight. If you're building a longer Morocco itinerary around imperial cities and the coast, a 7-day or longer route folds the Sahara into a complete circuit. And if you'd rather skip the well-worn Merzouga route entirely, ask us about Erg Chigaga — a quieter, more remote dune sea reached via Zagora. Our comparison of <a href="/blog-merzouga-zagora-erg-chigaga-agafay-which-desert">Merzouga, Zagora, Erg Chigaga and Agafay</a> explains the differences in detail.</p>
